{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Torah commanded to us by Moses.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): each member of the tribe of Levi may speak this utterance to Israel, and say to them: Thus we have transmitted from the mouth of Moses — a heritage in our hands, to us, for we are the congregation of Jacob. And he mentioned Jacob in connection with the Torah in order to exclude those who did not wish to accept it, and this is 'a heritage of the congregation of Jacob,' for it is not a heritage except to the seed of Jacob alone, because it is one of the four gifts that were given only to Israel alone." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that the word מורשה refers back to the land of Israel, and the meaning of the verse will be: by virtue of the Torah that Moses commanded us, the land concerning which it is written (Exodus 6:8) ״ונתתי אותה לכם מורשה אני ה׳״ — 'and I will give it to you as a heritage, I am YHWH' — shall become a heritage to the congregation of Jacob. For when we fulfill the Torah we merit inheriting it, and when we transgress it we are exiled and emptied of it. And this is the sense of מורשה from the language of (I Samuel 2:7) ״מוריש ומעשיר״ — 'He impoverishes and He enriches' — for it embraces two opposites, like (Numbers 4:13) ״ודשנו את המזבח״, for whether the congregation of Jacob inherits or is dispossessed, all depends on the Torah." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'Torah commanded to us by Moses' — commandments, as the numerical value of תור״ה צוה לנו משה, for we heard 'I' and 'You shall not have' from the mouth of the Almighty, and behold they are 613. 'A heritage of the congregation of Jacob' — to all who gather with Jacob, and these are the converts of righteousness. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: there are three crowns — the crown of Torah, the crown of priesthood, and the crown of kingship. The crown of priesthood was given to the seed of Aaron, the crown of kingship to the seed of David, the crown of Torah lies in the corner — whoever wishes to take it, let him come and take it. And they further interpreted it: 'a heritage (מורשה) of the congregation of Jacob' — do not read מורשה but מאורשה (betrothed). And by way of the Kabbalah it was necessary to interpret thus, for this Torah is betrothed to Jacob, and she is called Rachel, and in the time of anger, when the Shekhinah withdraws, Rachel weeps over her children, refusing to be comforted for her children because they are not; and in the time of favor, Rachel is the wife of Jacob. And this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :register {:flags {2 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {1 "Rabbeinu Bahya reads מורשה as an internally antithetical term (like מוריש ומעשיר), encompassing both inheritance and dispossession; the prooftext from Numbers 4:13 (ודשנו, 'they shall remove the ashes' from דשן 'fat/ashes') is cited as another word carrying opposed senses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:53:51.344116629Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}